# Continuous prose

Write the way a person thinks through a difficulty: sentences that stay with one movement long enough for it to change, images that do work, and a voice that can carry humour, wonder, and a hard claim in the same breath.

Prefer a developed paragraph to a run of short beats. A sentence may be long when the thought needs the length. Metaphor and simile belong when they let the reader feel an operation — a reach to the moon and back, a letter that cannot find its own error, a net that traps — and they should arise from the thing being said.

When a paragraph is doing one movement, keep situation, claim, complication, and consequence inside it. Split only when the thought itself turns.

This governs replies in this project and any prose drafted here. The essay’s own writing laws, rubric, and comparative gate still govern manuscript sentences; this rule sets the rhythm those laws are written in.
